**New Evidence**
- WHO’s 2022 Türkiye health-systems profile is the cleanest high-authority baseline on access and financial protection.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Health systems in action: Türkiye: 2022 edition","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022-09-07","url":"https://www.who.int/europe/publications/i/item/9789289059213","claim":"Türkiye's social health insurance now covers almost the entire resident population, including many refugees from Syria, and higher public spending with lower private spending has reduced out-of-pocket burden and catastrophic expenditure risk for households.","locator":"lines 259-261","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO European Observatory publication with direct country-specific system assessment and concrete coverage/financial-protection claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to 2022 and the 2022 ruler-period baseline.","ruler_attribution":"Partial but meaningful: the system predates 2022, yet Erdoğan's government was responsible for maintaining and directing it during the period.","contrary_evidence":["The same passage notes noncommunicable disease and antimicrobial-resistance challenges remained significant."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- WHO also documented substantial refugee-health strain in Türkiye during 2022, which matters for crisis handling and vulnerable-group access.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WHO Global Health Emergency Appeal: Turkey","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.who.int/emergencies/funding/health-emergency-appeals/2022/2023/2023-appeals/appeal-turkey","claim":"Türkiye hosted the world's largest refugee population, and unregistered foreigners had limited access to primary or referral health care while unmet health needs were worsened by the COVID-19 pandemic.","locator":"lines 223-226","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO emergency appeal page gives a direct, contemporaneous account of need and service gaps for refugees and migrants.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to the 2022 period and its social-service pressure.","ruler_attribution":"Partial: the refugee burden is inherited and geopolitical, but access design and ministry response were under Erdoğan-era authority.","contrary_evidence":["WHO also states it supported the Ministry of Health to provide essential services to refugee and host populations."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- UNICEF and the Turkish government transferred a major refugee child-support program in late 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Republic of Türkiye, the European Union, UNICEF and partners mark the transition of the Conditional Cash Transfer for Education (CCTE) for Refugees programme","publisher":"UNICEF Türkiye","publication_date":"2022-12-06","url":"https://www.unicef.org/turkiye/en/press-releases/republic-t%C3%BCrkiye-european-union-unicef-and-partners-mark-transition-conditional-cash","claim":"The CCTE for Refugees programme reached more than 811,000 children with regular cash support for education across 81 provinces and more than 170,000 children with child protection services in 15 provinces, and the programme was formally transferred to the Government of Türkiye.","locator":"lines 66-67","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF press release with specific beneficiary counts and a dated transfer of operational leadership to the government.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and about a 2022 institutional handoff.","ruler_attribution":"Partial but real: the transfer and implementation depended on the Turkish state, though donor and UNICEF support were material.","contrary_evidence":["The program remained donor-supported and not purely nationally financed."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The underlying evaluation shows the refugee cash-transfer system was not just symbolic; it delivered measurable attendance and protection outcomes.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Programme Evaluation of the Conditional Cash Transfer for Education (CCTE) for Syrians and Other Refugees in Turkey","publisher":"American Institutes for Research","publication_date":"2020-09","url":"https://www.air.org/sites/default/files/UNICEF-Turkey-CCTE-Evaluation-Report-September-2020.pdf","claim":"The evaluation found the program was an extension of the national CCTE launched in 2017, built on the Ministry of Family, Labour and Social Services' 2003 program for vulnerable families, and its child-protection component operated in 15 provinces.","locator":"pages 7-8, lines 179-206","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent mixed-methods evaluation with clear program history and institutional design detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective but directly informative about the 2022 welfare architecture and durability.","ruler_attribution":"Partial: the program predated 2022, but it reflects state social-policy architecture maintained under Erdoğan.","contrary_evidence":["The report notes the program was donor-supported and its child-protection arm had limited resources."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Programme Evaluation of the Conditional Cash Transfer for Education (CCTE) for Syrians and Other Refugees in Turkey","publisher":"American Institutes for Research","publication_date":"2020-09","url":"https://www.air.org/sites/default/files/UNICEF-Turkey-CCTE-Evaluation-Report-September-2020.pdf","claim":"The evaluation found regular attendance improved over time, the program never missed a transfer, and child-protection teams assisted 75,390 children between May 2017 and March 2020, with higher attendance in provinces with child-protection programming.","locator":"pages 9 and 73, lines 292-310 and 3229-3243","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent evaluation with concrete outcome findings and caveats about selection effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on a program still operative in 2022 and relevant to durability/outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Partial: the program itself was not created in 2022, but Erdoğan-era governance maintained and scaled it.","contrary_evidence":["The evaluation says the child-protection component still faced resource constraints and could not meet growing demand."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- UNICEF's program page provides a clear description of the refugee education cash-transfer design and its targeting of vulnerable children.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Conditional Cash Transfer for Education (CCTE) Programme","publisher":"UNICEF Türkiye","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://www.unicef.org/turkiye/en/conditional-cash-transfer-education-ccte-programme","claim":"Türkiye's national CCTE was extended to refugee children in 2017; families receive cash support every two months if children attend school regularly, and the child-protection component is implemented in all provinces with targeted outreach in 15 high-refugee provinces to mitigate vulnerability linked to economic hardship.","locator":"lines 78-93","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF program page is specific and internally consistent, but it is a program description rather than an independent audit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Program design remained operative in 2022 and explains the welfare architecture under Erdoğan.","ruler_attribution":"Partial: donor/UNICEF partnership mattered, but the national program sat inside the Turkish social-protection system.","contrary_evidence":["The page itself shows dependence on external funding and implementation partners."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.9"]}

- The macro shock in 2022 cut against ordinary households, especially poorer ones.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Global and Domestic Challenges Weigh on Outlook for Turkish Economy in 2022","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-02-25","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/02/25/global-and-domestic-challenges-weigh-on-outlook-for-turkish-economy-in-2022","claim":"Frequent monetary-policy changes and interest-rate cuts sent the lira to historic lows, drove inflation to record highs, and eroded real incomes for poorer households; the pandemic had also pushed an additional 1.6 million people below the $5.50 poverty line in 2020.","locator":"lines 143-146","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank country commentary with explicit poverty and real-income impacts tied to the 2022 policy environment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly addresses conditions at the start of 2022 and their social-welfare effects.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly partial-to-direct: the macro setting was shaped by Erdoğan-backed rate cuts and their social consequences.","contrary_evidence":["The same passage notes pandemic-related fiscal support softened the blow."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- OECD's 2022 outlook shows the government did use some targeted relief, even while the broader macro picture remained poor.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye: OECD Economic Outlook, Volume 2022 Issue 2","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-11-22","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2022/issue-2_f6da2159-en/full-report/component-51.html","claim":"The government introduced measures to shield vulnerable groups from rising energy and food prices, the number of households receiving energy assistance doubled to 4 million, and electricity tariffs for low-consumption households were cut.","locator":"lines 4046-4047","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD surveillance note gives a concrete policy measure with quantified coverage and direct distributional framing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 policy response.","ruler_attribution":"Partial: this was Erdoğan-era policy, though the need arose from the inflation shock and energy-price environment.","contrary_evidence":["The same OECD chapter warns that households in the lowest decile spend nearly 70% of their budget on food and housing, and inflation remained above 40%."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- Türkiye's headline human-development picture was strong by 2022 data, but the gender-inequality adjustment shows a meaningful distribution/dignity gap.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye ranks 45th of 193 countries in human development, new UNDP report says","publisher":"United Nations Development Programme","publication_date":"2024-03-13","url":"https://www.undp.org/turkiye/press-releases/turkiye-ranks-45th-193-countries-human-development-new-undp-report-says","claim":"Based on 2022 data, Türkiye's HDI was 0.855, in the 'very high human development' category, with life expectancy, schooling, and GNI per capita all higher than in 1990.","locator":"lines 43-49","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNDP press release summarizes the 2022 dataset and long-run human-development change in a direct, citable way.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 data; useful for end-state and trajectory.","ruler_attribution":"Partial: long-run improvements predate and exceed Erdoğan alone, but the 2022 end-state is part of his inherited-and-maintained baseline.","contrary_evidence":["The same release says the ranking is based on 2022 data, so it does not reflect the 2023 earthquakes."],"lenses":["6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye ranks 45th of 193 countries in human development, new UNDP report says","publisher":"United Nations Development Programme","publication_date":"2024-03-13","url":"https://www.undp.org/turkiye/press-releases/turkiye-ranks-45th-193-countries-human-development-new-undp-report-says","claim":"When HDI is adjusted for inequality, Türkiye's ranking falls to 63rd of 166 countries, and men enjoy a higher level of human development than women.","locator":"line 49","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same UNDP release includes a clear inequality-adjusted comparison and gender gap warning.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 data; directly relevant to distribution and dignity.","ruler_attribution":"Partial: this reflects structural inequality under the Erdoğan-era state, but not all of it is uniquely attributable to 2022 decisions.","contrary_evidence":["The same release also emphasizes strong overall HDI progress over the long run."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- WHO's Istanbul meeting on refugee and migrant health is useful as context on state cooperation and service continuity.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Report on the High-level Meeting on Health and Migration: 17–18 March 2022, Istanbul","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022-07-15","url":"https://www.who.int/europe/publications/i/item/WHO-EURO-2022-5833-45598-65403","claim":"The 2022 Istanbul meeting brought together governments, partner agencies, and civil society to set priorities for refugee and migrant health, and the outcome statement unanimously endorsed five pillars of action to make health a reality for all.","locator":"lines 259-261","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO technical document is a solid record of a policy forum, though it is more about coordination than direct delivery.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and in Türkiye.","ruler_attribution":"Partial/contextual: it reflects government-hosted coordination rather than a single ruler-specific welfare act.","contrary_evidence":["A meeting is not the same as implementation, so this is weaker than the delivery evidence above."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.6","6B.9"]}

- UNICEF's education-system page adds broader institutional context on inclusion for vulnerable children.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Systems-strengthening, inclusive basic education and learning","publisher":"UNICEF Türkiye","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://www.unicef.org/turkiye/en/systems-strengthening-inclusive-basic-education-and-learning","claim":"UNICEF says Türkiye has vulnerable children including refugees, children with disabilities, and children living in poverty, and that it works with the government on literacy, numeracy, special education, counselling services, and communication support for Syrian families.","locator":"page text shown in search snippet and page body summary","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF program page is directly relevant to inclusion and service design, but it is a general program description rather than a dated audit.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Program context relevant to 2022 welfare institutions.","ruler_attribution":"Partial/contextual: it describes an ongoing government-UNICEF partnership inside the Turkish system.","contrary_evidence":["The page does not by itself show 2022 delivery or outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.9"]}

- UNICEF's early-childhood page gives another inclusion-focused institutional angle, especially for the most vulnerable and low pre-primary enrollment.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Early Childhood Education","publisher":"UNICEF Türkiye","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://www.unicef.org/turkiye/en/early-childhood-education","claim":"UNICEF says Türkiye still has one of the lowest net pre-primary enrolment rates among OECD countries and that it prioritizes accessible, inclusive, good-quality early learning for the most vulnerable children while working with the Ministry of National Education to expand good-quality spaces.","locator":"page summary in search result","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF program page is clear on priorities and the baseline challenge, but it is not a year-specific evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Context for 2022 social-policy conditions and durable institutional goals.","ruler_attribution":"Partial/contextual: the page reflects ongoing ministry-UNICEF cooperation, not a single 2022 decision.","contrary_evidence":["It says the challenge is persistent, which limits claims of decisive 2022 success."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.9","6B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I searched broadly across roughly 25-40 documents, including WHO Europe country health material, WHO emergency appeals, UNICEF Türkiye program pages and press releases, AIR's independent evaluation PDF, UNDP's 2022/2024 human-development releases, World Bank Turkey economic monitor material, OECD surveillance, and a smaller number of UNHCR/refugee-education pages.
- I kept the strongest non-duplicative items that actually said something material about 2022 welfare choices, delivery, or distribution.
- I rejected or deprioritized broader UNICEF/UNHCR pages when they were too generic, too post-period, or redundant with a stronger source on the same mechanism.
- I also rejected 2023 earthquake-response material as out of period for this request, except where a page was only being considered for general institutional context and not used as final evidence.
- I did not keep pure outcome snapshots without a ruler link when a better source already showed both the policy mechanism and the welfare effect.
- I avoided reusing the same passage through multiple mirrors unless the mirror added a materially distinct claim or a better locator.

**Remaining Gaps**
- The strongest evidence is on health coverage, refugee-access systems, and child cash-transfer programs; I did not find equally strong 2022 evidence for housing, sanitation, or food-security policy that was both ruler-specific and not redundant.
- Distributional evidence is better for refugees, children, and low-income households than for women, disabled people, or rural regions specifically.
- The record still leans on donor-UNICEF/WHO partnerships in several places, so Erdoğan attribution is meaningful but not exclusive.
- More search would help if you want tighter evidence on direct Turkish-budget social spending, school-quality outcomes, or how much of the welfare architecture in 2022 was Erdoğan-directed versus inherited system maintenance.
